Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:06.090
80% of your time, more 90% of your
time, chasing 10% of the words.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.710
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.430
Uh, today I want to talk, of course about
language learning, but I want to talk

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:16.800
about the 80/20 rule or Pareto's Principle
as it applies to language learning.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.240
Remember, if you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, click on the bell for

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.340
notifications, and if you follow me on a
podcast service, please leave a comment.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.360
I do appreciate it.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.580
We've all, I shouldn't say we all, but
many of us are familiar with the idea

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:38.250
that, let's say if you're in a company,
uh, a small number of customers will

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.680
account for most of your business.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:46.290
You know, uh, a small number of
criminals do most of the crime.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.710
Uh, there are many situations, if
you Google uh, Pareto's Principle.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:59.910
You will find all kinds of examples
of how a small number of any sort

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:05.835
of series of events will account for
the lion's share of these events.

00:01:05.895 --> 00:01:09.465
So people say, well, this also
applies to language learning.

00:01:09.795 --> 00:01:12.914
By the way, I should point
out that there was a comment

00:01:12.914 --> 00:01:14.505
about the quality of my audio.

00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:16.274
I'm always sensitive to this.

00:01:16.274 --> 00:01:18.435
I want to make sure I do the best I can.

00:01:18.884 --> 00:01:22.455
Uh, I've tried a number of
different speakers, microphones

00:01:22.455 --> 00:01:28.125
rather, and it always seems that
the best sound comes from my Mac.

00:01:28.905 --> 00:01:33.045
And so I discontinued using these
other microphones, but then I had the

00:01:33.045 --> 00:01:38.295
criticism again, so I decided that I
would plug this, uh, uh, microphone

00:01:38.295 --> 00:01:40.335
in and see if the sound is any better.

00:01:40.545 --> 00:01:41.895
Please let me know.

00:01:42.195 --> 00:01:47.655
Now, maybe again, it's a small
number of people complaining who

00:01:48.165 --> 00:01:49.485
account for all the complaints.

00:01:49.755 --> 00:01:53.025
However, if I can actually
improve the quality of the

00:01:53.025 --> 00:01:54.735
audio, I'm very happy to do so.

00:01:55.035 --> 00:02:00.945
So you often hear people say, and it's
quite true to say that a small number

00:02:00.945 --> 00:02:06.220
of words, a thousand words, depending
on, you know, whatever statistics

00:02:06.765 --> 00:02:14.445
are used, 500 words, a thousand words
account for, let's say 70% of the

00:02:14.445 --> 00:02:18.060
word count in a given bit of content.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.200
Now, first of all, let's say that
this Pareto Principle 80/20 rule,

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.720
it doesn't mean exactly 80 and 20.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.420
It can be 70/30, it can be 90/10,
but it just means that a small number

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:37.500
of problems account for most of the
inconvenience that we experience.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.859
Maybe in language learning, you make
the same mistakes over and over again.

00:02:40.859 --> 00:02:44.820
So it's a small number of grammar
problems that give you most of

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.460
your difficulty, let's say with
the language you're learning.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:52.680
And people say, you know, if you
just learn the hundred most common

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:58.140
words, then you'll be able to
deal with most language context.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:03.735
Unfortunately, when it comes to
language learning, that's not the case.

00:03:04.125 --> 00:03:09.345
In other words, you can know and it's
relatively easy to get to know the most

00:03:09.345 --> 00:03:13.875
common thousand words because they show
up, you know, as I've said before, the

00:03:13.875 --> 00:03:15.675
frequency is very high with these words.

00:03:15.765 --> 00:03:19.755
You get to know them pretty quickly,
uh, as long as you continue studying.

00:03:20.115 --> 00:03:26.995
Unfortunately, the other 30%,
even 20% of words that you need in

00:03:26.995 --> 00:03:32.130
order to understand any meaningful
context is a very large number.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.870
And so in English, or at least in
language learning, you end up spending

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:44.580
basically 80% of your time, more, 90%
of your time chasing 10% of the words.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.610
So it's kind of an
inverse Pareto Principle.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.730
And to illustrate this,
I'll show you my statistics.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:57.959
So let's look at how the pareto
Principle, or really the reverse Pareto

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:01.049
Principle applies to language learning.

00:04:01.049 --> 00:04:06.279
If I look at, say, Farsi, which is
where I am right now, and I go to my

00:04:06.285 --> 00:04:12.510
profile, and I recommend that you do
that from time to time, uh, if I look

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:19.200
at sort of my known words for the last,
uh, you know, all time, for example,

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:24.179
uh, I have say 12,000 known words.

00:04:24.719 --> 00:04:35.190
However, um, and, and I have, if I look
at LingQs created, uh, I have, um, 32,000.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:38.969
So 32,000 words I have attempted to learn.

00:04:38.969 --> 00:04:40.859
Most of them I still haven't learned.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:48.159
However, if we say that a thousand
words accounts for 60, 70% of any given

00:04:48.419 --> 00:04:55.905
context, the overwhelming majority of
my words, uh, are words that are outside

00:04:55.905 --> 00:05:01.965
the sort of scope of those most frequent,
you know, that Pareto Principle should

00:05:01.965 --> 00:05:04.155
account for the bulk of any context.

00:05:04.155 --> 00:05:05.775
I need all these words.

00:05:06.135 --> 00:05:11.535
And similarly, if I change this to,
let's take a Czech, for example.

00:05:12.105 --> 00:05:16.575
Uh, I mean, I've created
60,000 LingQs in Czech, 60,000.

00:05:17.234 --> 00:05:23.325
And probably the most common thousand
words, uh, will account for the bulk

00:05:23.325 --> 00:05:25.005
of any text that I'm gonna read.

00:05:25.005 --> 00:05:26.835
But I need all these other words.

00:05:26.835 --> 00:05:30.675
And I could show you this in every
language that I've learned on LingQ.

00:05:31.005 --> 00:05:33.164
And I, I think it applies to all of us.

00:05:33.255 --> 00:05:35.054
We need a lot of words.

00:05:35.445 --> 00:05:40.065
And so the most frequent words,
it's nice to think that you're gonna

00:05:40.065 --> 00:05:45.044
have 60, 70% of any context, uh, but
you're gonna learn those words anyway.

00:05:45.044 --> 00:05:48.974
So the idea that you can ace the
language by focusing on the most common

00:05:48.974 --> 00:05:54.044
words according to some kind of Pareto
Principle actually is not the case.

00:05:54.914 --> 00:05:59.505
You know, if, if we first of all
accept the fact that the thousand most

00:05:59.895 --> 00:06:05.354
frequent words in any language are
going to amount or, or cover, you know,

00:06:05.445 --> 00:06:12.030
60, 70, 80% of the word count in any
text, depending on the nature of the

00:06:12.035 --> 00:06:16.440
text, depending on the language, but
there's a very high proportion of high

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.980
frequency words that show up in any text.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:25.680
Uh, however, as I've said before, the
frequency declines very, very quickly.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:33.525
But you look at, at my numbers, uh,
for Arabic, for Czech for literally any

00:06:33.935 --> 00:06:37.785
language that I've learned at LingQ, and
you can go and do this yourself, go look

00:06:37.785 --> 00:06:42.045
through if you're studying one language
or several languages, and you'll see

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:50.115
just how large a vocabulary you need in
order to be fluent in order to start to

00:06:50.115 --> 00:06:52.975
understand interesting content in, in...

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.765
from books or from movies
or from any source.

00:06:56.145 --> 00:07:01.080
Uh, maybe content that you need for your
work, you're gonna find that you need...

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:07.320
even for movies where the
vocabulary level is lower.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:11.010
But if you get into technical
subjects, the vocabulary

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.170
required is very specialized.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.980
And in order to acquire this
vocabulary, you have to do a

00:07:16.985 --> 00:07:17.970
lot of reading and listening.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:24.555
So you end up spending 90% of your time
pursuing, you know, in the case of, I, I'm

00:07:24.555 --> 00:07:28.575
going from memory here, but if, if even
in Czech, I think I have 60,000 words,

00:07:28.905 --> 00:07:33.435
but the most frequent thousand words
will account for most of the content.

00:07:33.435 --> 00:07:36.015
So there is no shortcut.

00:07:36.045 --> 00:07:37.275
I guess that's the message.

00:07:37.275 --> 00:07:40.905
There is no shortcut because
language is about learning words.

00:07:41.414 --> 00:07:46.245
I haven't, I tried to Google to find
out if there's some sense of how many

00:07:46.424 --> 00:07:51.104
grammatical structures or patterns there
are in a given, you know, in English

00:07:51.104 --> 00:07:55.424
or in other languages, and how many
of them tend to show up all the time,

00:07:55.424 --> 00:08:00.465
and how many of them are less frequent
or conversely either certain patterns

00:08:00.525 --> 00:08:05.174
that give the greatest difficulty
to learners of a certain language.

00:08:05.205 --> 00:08:09.495
And, and that might be an example of the
Pareto Principle, you know, maybe ser and

00:08:09.495 --> 00:08:15.975
estar in Spanish continue to give a lot
of difficulty or, uh, in Russian or in

00:08:15.975 --> 00:08:20.445
Slavic languages, the case endings or,
uh, you know, other things like this.

00:08:20.505 --> 00:08:27.645
So, but the structures that cause
the most difficulty may also be the

00:08:27.645 --> 00:08:33.495
structures that show up the most often,
and maybe it's just through continuing

00:08:33.495 --> 00:08:38.235
to listen and read and allowing a reign
gradually get to, to get used to these

00:08:38.235 --> 00:08:39.674
structures that you get better at them.

00:08:39.914 --> 00:08:44.324
Because I've had the experience of reading
the explanations over and over again and

00:08:44.324 --> 00:08:51.990
still not being able to nail, you know,
case endings in Russian or, um, you know,

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:53.640
verbs of motion or this kind of thing.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:57.420
So I just wanted to raise this
issue and I'd be interested in,

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.090
in, uh, the reaction from people.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.250
Uh, to what extent is this
Pareto Principle applicable?

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:06.750
Is there something that I'm missing here?

00:09:06.939 --> 00:09:10.180
Is there some way that we can take
advantage of the Pareto Principle

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.810
to speed up our language learning?

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:17.430
Unfortunately, I suspect that this is
not the case, and I thought I'd leave you

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:22.950
with a couple of videos that you can, uh,
you know, add to or follow up on if you

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:24.540
want more information on this subject.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:29.460
I did one on the Pareto, Pareto Principle
some while ago, which you can check out.

00:09:29.925 --> 00:09:38.055
And again, uh, part of my sort of, what I
find so important in this on the subject

00:09:38.055 --> 00:09:40.694
is that we do need a lot of words.

00:09:40.905 --> 00:09:44.145
So please check out the video that
I did on that subject as well.

00:09:44.625 --> 00:09:45.225
